[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should the shared store hold after a successful login? → Identity only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Identity only — good, that keeps the token question off the table.

  Next: how long it survives. [1mThe fork[0m is what happens when the user reloads or opens a second tab.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives reload and browser restart, shared across all tabs. Matches how a real session cookie
    behaves, so tabs agree with each other. Cost: it can go stale — the server session expires but the page still shows
    someone logged in until something corrects it.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reload, but is per-tab and dies with the tab. No stale-across-days problem. Cost: open a
    second tab and the app looks logged out even though the session is valid, which reads as a bug to users.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module variable, gone on reload. Simplest and never stale, but it isn't persistence, and you
    asked for persistence.

  Either storage choice is cheap to swap later [3mif[0m reads and writes go through one module rather than being sprinkled
  across call sites — which is the main thing the design should buy you. What isn't cheap to retrofit is treating the
  stored identity as authoritative; that assumption spreads into every consumer.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, with the stored identity explicitly documented as a cache whose source of truth is
  the server. Cross-tab consistency is the behavior users expect, and the staleness risk is bounded because nothing
  security-relevant is stored — worst case is a wrong name on screen until the next server call corrects it.
